Ladybird now hands a per-site RequestServer its site over IPC, before
any client connects, and none of its processes names a site on its
command line. Chrome 158, Firefox 161 and Safari 26.6.2 (WebKit) were
each run in a macOS 26.6.2 VM with example.com and wikipedia.org open,
and ps showed no site in the arguments of any helper or content process
of theirs. Chrome’s single network.mojom.NetworkService utility serves
every site, and its renderers carry only flags (--renderer-client-id,
e.g.). Firefox’s content processes carry only flags and their “tab”
process type; Gecko’s ContentParent sends the remote type, which names
the site of a Fission process, in a SetRemoteType message once the
process is up. And WebKit’s com.apple.WebKit.Networking and WebContent
XPC services run with no arguments at all.
